// A recipe, written from md-docs/recipes/check-a-deploy.md by scripts/docsite/recipes.py;
// edit the recipe, not this file.
page RecipeCheckADeploy(path: "/recipes/check-a-deploy", title: "Check a deploy — Recipes", description: "Check a WebFluent site after each deploy: deep links that reload, text in the HTML, the response headers, link previews and the sitemap.") {
    RecipeShell("Check a deploy", summary: "This recipe is the list to go through after each deploy, on any host: the mistakes it catches pass every check on your own machine.  You need the deployed site's address.  - Open a deep link directly and reload it. A 404 means the host does not fall back to `index.html` (a single-page build), or `base_path` is wrong. - View a page's source. In a static build, the page's text is in the HTML. - Check the response headers in the browser's network panel. - Run a link-preview debugger on a page, and submit `sitemap.xml` to the search engines' consoles.  The same checks from a terminal, with your site's address in `SITE` and a page other than the home page in place of `/docs`:", outputs: ["website", "app"], since: "", recipes: RECIPE_INDEX, sections: [Anchor(id: "how-it-works", label: "How it works")], related: [RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Set cache and security headers", to: "/recipes/cache-and-security-headers"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Describe pages to search engines and link previews", to: "/recipes/search-and-link-previews"), RelatedLink(kind: "Recipe", title: "Install wf on a build machine", to: "/recipes/install-wf-in-ci")], source: "md-docs/recipes/check-a-deploy.md", how: true, hasVariations: false, project: {}) {
        code {
            Terminal(lines: [
                        Line(command: "SITE=https://example.com"),
                        Line(command: "curl -sI \"$SITE/docs\" | head -1                     # HTTP/2 200, not 404"),
                        Line(command: "curl -s \"$SITE/docs\" | grep -o '<h1[^>]*>[^<]*'     # a static build: the heading is in the HTML"),
                        Line(command: "curl -sI \"$SITE/\" | grep -iE 'content-security-policy|x-frame-options|cache-control'"),
                        Line(command: "curl -s \"$SITE/sitemap.xml\" | head                  # absolute addresses under meta.site_url")
                    ])
        }
        Markdown("Each line prints something when the deploy is right: a `200` status, the page's `<h1>` and its text, the three headers, and the first entries of the sitemap. An empty line is the check that failed.\n\nFor the link preview, paste a page's address into the [Facebook Sharing Debugger](https://developers.facebook.com/tools/debug/) or [LinkedIn's Post Inspector](https://www.linkedin.com/post-inspector/); for the sitemap, submit `$SITE/sitemap.xml` in [Google Search Console](https://search.google.com/search-console) and [Bing Webmaster Tools](https://www.bing.com/webmasters).\n\n- A deep link loaded directly is the one request the host answers without the router: the home page can work while every other address is a 404.\n- A static build pre-renders every page, so its text should be in the HTML the host sends ([Static sites and SPAs](/about/static-or-single-page)).\n- The headers show whether the host sends what `build/_headers` holds ([Set cache and security headers](/recipes/cache-and-security-headers)).\n- A link preview and the sitemap use the absolute addresses `meta.site_url` names ([Describe pages to search engines and link previews](/recipes/search-and-link-previews)).\n\nBefore the next deploy, `wf verify` loads every page of the build in headless Chrome and catches a script error, a failed request or a blank page before it ships ([Install wf on a build machine](/recipes/install-wf-in-ci)).")

    }
}
